{"name":"Prism","hex":"#aadccd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"prism","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A polyhedron with parallel ends of the same polygonal shape and size, the other faces being parallelogram-shaped sides.\nAn object having the shape of a geometrical prism (sense 1).\n A cutting (“open passage at a level lower than the surrounding terrain, dug for a canal, railway, or road to go through”) or an embankment shaped like a prism (sense 1) or a number of prisms, such that its volume can be easily calculated.\n A crystal in which the faces are parallel to the vertical axis.\n A transparent block in the shape of a prism (sense 1), typically with triangular ends, used to reflect or refract light.\n\n A spectrum produced by light being refracted through a prism.","An object having the shape of a geometrical prism (sense 1)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/prism"}],"entries":[{"title":"Prism (optics)","blocks":[{"text":"An optical prism is a transparent optical element with flat, polished surfaces that are designed to refract light. At least one surface must be angled—elements with only two parallel surfaces are windows, not prisms. The most familiar type of optical prism is the triangular prism, which has a triangular base and rectangular sides. Not all optical prisms are geometric prisms, and not all geometric prisms would count as an optical prism. Prisms can be made from any material that is transparent to the wavelengths for which they are designed. Typical materials include glass, acrylic and fluorite."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Dispersive_prism.png/330px-Dispersive_prism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prism_(optics)","wikidata":"Q165896"},{"title":"Prism (geometry)","blocks":[{"text":"In geometry, a prism is a polyhedron comprising an n-sided polygon base, a second base which is a translated copy of the first, and n other faces, necessarily all parallelograms, joining corresponding sides of the two bases. All cross-sections parallel to the bases are translations of the bases. Prisms are named after their bases, e.g. a prism with a pentagonal base is called a pentagonal prism. Prisms are a subclass of prismatoids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Hexagonal_Prism_BC.svg/330px-Hexagonal_Prism_BC.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prism_(geometry)","wikidata":"Q180544"},{"title":"PRISM","blocks":[{"text":"PRISM is a code name for a program under which the United States National Security Agency (NSA) collects internet communications from various U.S. internet companies. The program is also known by the SIGAD US-984XN. PRISM collects stored internet communications based on demands made to internet companies such as Google and Apple under Section 702 of the FISA Amendments Act of 2008 to turn over any data that match court-approved search terms. Among other things, the NSA can use these PRISM requests to target communications that were encrypted when they traveled across the internet backbone, to focus on stored data that telecommunication filtering systems discarded earlier, and to get data that is easier to handle."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Boundless_Informant_data_collection_-_DNI.svg/330px-Boundless_Informant_data_collection_-_DNI.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PRISM","wikidata":"Q13420597"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Twisted_square_antiprism.png/500px-Twisted_square_antiprism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Twisted_square_antiprism.png/500px-Twisted_square_antiprism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATwisted_square_antiprism.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Square_antiprism.png/500px-Square_antiprism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Square_antiprism.png/500px-Square_antiprism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASquare_antiprism.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Twisted_dodecagonal_antiprism.png/500px-Twisted_dodecagonal_antiprism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Twisted_dodecagonal_antiprism.png/1280px-Twisted_dodecagonal_antiprism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Twisted_dodecagonal_antiprism.png/500px-Twisted_dodecagonal_antiprism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATwisted_dodecagonal_antiprism.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Crossed-square_prism.png/500px-Crossed-square_prism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Crossed-square_prism.png/1280px-Crossed-square_prism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Crossed-square_prism.png/500px-Crossed-square_prism.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrossed-square_p